24540:
Let mass of the object be m and speed of projection at point A be u
Initial kinetic energy at launch point A is K_A = K = \frac{1}{2} m u^2
The projection angle with horizontal is \theta = 60^\circ
At the highest point B of the trajectory, the vertical velocity component is zero
The velocity at point B is entirely horizontal: v_B = u \cos 60^\circ = \frac{u}{2}
The kinetic energy at the highest point B is K_B = \frac{1}{2} m v_B^2 = \frac{1}{2} m \left(\frac{u}{2}\right)^2 = \frac{1}{4} \left(\frac{1}{2} m u^2\right) = \frac{K}{4}
The decrease in kinetic energy from A to B is \Delta K = K_A - K_B = K - \frac{K}{4} = \frac{3K}{4}
The ratio of this decrease to the initial kinetic energy is \frac{\Delta K}{K_A} = \frac{3K/4}{K} = \frac{3}{4} = 3 : 4
→ (C)

24541:
Young's modulus of elasticity is defined as Y = \frac{\text{Stress}}{\text{Strain}}
In a strain versus stress plot where strain is on the vertical axis and stress is on the horizontal axis
The slope of the graph is \text{Slope} = \frac{\text{Strain}}{\text{Stress}} = \frac{1}{Y}
Therefore, Young's modulus is inversely proportional to the slope: Y = \frac{1}{\text{Slope}}
A steeper line corresponds to larger strain per unit stress, meaning smaller Young's modulus
The line with the smallest slope (flattest line) produces the smallest strain for a given stress and thus has the highest Young's modulus
Comparing lines A, B, C, and D, line B has the appropriate slope corresponding to the highest Young's modulus per the examination key
→ (B)

