24590:
The integral is f(x) = \int \frac{2x e^x}{(1 + x)^{3/2}} dx with f(0) = 0
Write the numerator as 2x = 2(1 + x) - 2:
\frac{2x}{(1 + x)^{3/2}} = \frac{2(1 + x)}{(1 + x)^{3/2}} - \frac{2}{(1 + x)^{3/2}} = \frac{2}{\sqrt{1 + x}} - \frac{2}{(1 + x)^{3/2}}
Notice that \frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac{2}{\sqrt{1 + x}}\right) = 2\left(-\frac{1}{2}\right)(1 + x)^{-3/2} = -\frac{1}{(1 + x)^{3/2}}
This is of the standard form \int e^x [g(x) + g'(x)] dx = e^x g(x) + C with g(x) = \frac{2}{\sqrt{1 + x}}
Therefore f(x) = \frac{2 e^x}{\sqrt{1 + x}} + C
Using initial condition f(0) = 0: f(0) = \frac{2 e^0}{\sqrt{1}} + C = 2 + C = 0 \implies C = -2
So f(x) = \frac{2 e^x}{\sqrt{1 + x}} - 2
Now evaluate at x = 1: f(1) = \frac{2 e^1}{\sqrt{1 + 1}} - 2 = \frac{2e}{\sqrt{2}} - 2 = \sqrt{2} e - 2
→ (B)
